// Runtime clothing pixel-remap system.
//
// Problem: a species/leg-type combo with a non-default body silhouette (e.g. Anthro's digitigrade legs, or a
// whole non-human species like Unathi/Nabber/Monarch Queen) needs every leg-covering clothing item to visually
// match that silhouette. Hand-drawing a matching sprite for every single item is the "content debt" this session
// flagged early on -- this system avoids that by warping the item's EXISTING (baseline human-drawn) sprite at
// render time instead, pixel by pixel, rather than needing new art per item.
//
// The mapping data itself is NOT hand-derived or guessed -- it's authored visually by the user via a reference
// DMI (icons/mob/human_races/species/human/reference.dmi): a "reference-<category>" state (the baseline source,
// each opaque pixel perturbed to a unique near-identical color so individual pixels can be told apart) and one
// "<shape_key>-<category>" state per target body shape (the same pixels manually repositioned by hand to that
// shape's silhouette -- e.g. "unathi-uniform", "vox-boots"). Matching pixel colors between the source and each
// target gives an exact list of (source x,y) -> (dest x,y) moves per direction, independently per target. Some
// source pixels legitimately have no match in a given output (dropped -- fine, not every pixel needs to survive
// every shape) and some appear at multiple destinations (duplicated -- fine, used for extra coverage/
// expressiveness). get_leg_remap_pixels() below is that extracted table, regenerated by re-running the
// extraction script in the scratchpad against a new/updated reference.dmi rather than hand-editing these
// coordinate lists.
//
// One rule is defined per (clothing category, body shape key) pair and reused by every item in that category --
// set /obj/item/clothing/var/leg_remap_category on a shared parent type (see leg_remap_category in _clothing.dm)
// rather than per-leaf-item, so e.g. "pants" only needs one rule PER SHAPE to cover every pants-like item at
// once. Since each mapping is defined purely in terms of SOURCE COORDINATES, an item that doesn't have art at
// some source pixel (e.g. shorts not covering the lower leg at all) just contributes nothing for that entry --
// whatever pixels the item DOES have still get transposed, matching the "transpose as many of the original
// pixels as you can" rule. CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_SKIRT is additive on top of PANTS rather than a full rule of its
// own: get_leg_remapped_icon() combines a skirt's own (small) table with the PANTS table for the same shape_key,
// since a jumpskirt is a jumpsuit's silhouette plus a bit more, not an unrelated shape (2026-09-03; supersedes an
// earlier decision to skip skirts entirely and just reuse PANTS unmodified, before reference-skirt existed).
//
// get_body_shape_key() (on the human mob) decides which shape key applies to a given wearer -- "digitigrade" for
// Anthro (leg_type dependent, since Anthro can choose plantigrade instead), "unathi"/"gas"/"msq"/"teshari"/"vox"
// unconditionally for those species. "teshari" activated alongside the Teshari species port (2026-09-03) --
// Teshari didn't exist as a BS12SOYH species before that. "vox" activated in the same pass: this mapping was
// drawn against NovaSector's `better_vox` art specifically, and that art was ported into this codebase's own Vox
// species (icons/mob/human_races/species/vox/body.dmi) as part of the same session, so the mapping now matches
// the live Vox body shape rather than being for a hypothetical future alternate Vox.

// Cache of built remap icons, keyed by "[icon]-[state]-[category]-[shape_key]" -- these are expensive to build
// (up to a few hundred single-pixel Crop+Blend calls per direction) but static per (item art, category, shape),
// so build once and reuse for every mob of that shape wearing that same item.
GLOBAL_LIST_EMPTY(leg_remap_icon_cache)

// Auto-generated from icons/mob/human_races/species/human/reference.dmi's reference-<category> -> <shape_key>-
// <category> pixel mappings (user-authored, not hand-derived -- see this file's header comment). Returns a flat
// list of list(source_x, source_y, dest_x, dest_y) entries (BYOND icon coordinates: 1-indexed, origin bottom-
// left) for one (category, shape_key, dir) combo, or null if that combo has no rule for that direction.
// Split into several sub-procedures purely for BYOND's compiled-bytecode size limit on a single
// procedure ("Procedure is too large") -- this dispatcher just routes to whichever one actually has the
// category being asked for. Grouped by rough theme (leg-shape / accessories+bags / armor), not by anything
// semantically meaningful -- add new categories to whichever group has headroom, or start a new group.
/proc/get_leg_remap_pixels(category, shape_key, dir)
	switch(category)
		if(CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_PANTS, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_BOOTS, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_SKIRT)
			return get_leg_remap_pixels_legshape(category, shape_key, dir)
		if(CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_INSIGNIA, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_RANK, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_TIE, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_BACKPACK, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_RUCKSACK, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_SATCHEL, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_MESSENGERBAG, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_POCKETBOOK, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_DUFFLEBAG)
			return get_leg_remap_pixels_accessories(category, shape_key, dir)
		if(CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_HELMET, CLOTHING_LEG_CATEGORY_SUIT)
			return get_leg_remap_pixels_armor(category, shape_key, dir)
	return null

